## The work — a checklist over the existing folder

### ➕ Add — `boukensha/agent.py`

Class constants, mirroring Ruby:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `MAX_ITERATIONS = 25` | same (note this duplicates `Tasks::Base::DEFAULT_MAX_ITERATIONS`; Ruby carries both, so carry both) |
| `WRAP_UP_OUTPUT_TOKENS = 400` | same |
| `WRAP_UP_DIRECTIVE = <<~MSG.strip` | a triple-quoted string, `.strip()`-equivalent content. The squiggly heredoc strips leading indentation, so the stored value is three lines with **no** leading spaces and no trailing newline — reproduce that exactly, since it is sent to the model |

Constructor and methods:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `initialize(context:, registry:, builder:, client:, task_settings: nil, max_iterations: nil, max_output_tokens: nil)` | `def __init__(self, *, context, registry, builder, client, task_settings=None, max_iterations=None, max_output_tokens=None)` |
| `@iteration = 0` | `self.iteration = 0` |
| `explicit.to_i unless explicit.nil?` | `int(explicit) if explicit is not None` |
| `@context.task.respond_to?(:max_iterations)` | `hasattr(self.context.task, "max_iterations")` |
| `@max_iterations.positive? && @iteration >= @max_iterations` | `self.max_iterations > 0 and self.iteration >= self.max_iterations` |
| `content.select { \|b\| b["type"] == "text" }.map { \|b\| b["text"] }.join` | `"".join(b["text"] for b in content if b["type"] == "text")` |
| `text.strip.empty?` | `not text.strip()` |
| `rescue ApiError` | `except ApiError:` |
| `result.to_s[0..60]` | `str(result)[:61]` — **inclusive range → 61** |
| `puts "[iteration #{@iteration}/#{@max_iterations}]"` | `print(f"[iteration {self.iteration}/{self.max_iterations}]")` |

> ⚠️ **Two truthiness traps, and the second one silently breaks the wind-down.**
>
> `call_opts` is `@max_output_tokens ? { max_output_tokens: … } : {}`. In Ruby
> `0` is truthy, so an explicit `max_output_tokens: 0` is forwarded. Written as
> `if self.max_output_tokens:` in Python, zero would be dropped. Use
> `is not None`.
>
> The worse one is in the backends (below): `tools.nil? ? to_tools(…) : tools`.
> `wrap_up` deliberately calls `client.call(tools: [], …)` — an **empty list** —
> to disable tools for the final message. Ported as `tools or self.to_tools(…)`,
> the empty list is falsy, the full tool set comes back, and the model can keep
> calling tools during the very call meant to stop it. The iteration ceiling
> then does nothing. It must be `to_tools(…) if tools is None else tools`.

`wrap_up` runs **outside** the counted loop: it does not increment `iteration`
and never re-checks the limit, so it cannot re-trigger itself. Preserve that.

### ✏️ Edit — all five backends

Each gains `tools=None` on `to_payload`, using the `is None` test above, and a
`parse_response` that normalizes to:

```python
{"stop_reason": "tool_use" | "end_turn",
 "content": [{"type": "text", "text": …},
             {"type": "tool_use", "id": …, "name": …, "input": {…}}]}
```

Note the **mixed key convention**, which Ruby has and Python must match on the
wire: the outer dict uses symbol-ish keys (`stop_reason`, `content` — plain
strings in Python) while the inner blocks use string keys. In Python both are
strings, so the distinction disappears — one less thing to think about, but do
not "tidy" the inner blocks into anything else, because `Agent` and
`assistant_message` both index them by `"type"`, `"text"`, `"id"`, `"name"`,
`"input"`.

Per backend:

| Backend | `parse_response` reads | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Anthropic | `response["stop_reason"]`, `response["content"]` | `stop_reason` is normalized to exactly `"tool_use"` or `"end_turn"` — anything else collapses to `"end_turn"`. **No `assistant_message` needed**: Anthropic's `content` array *is* the normalized shape, which is why its `to_messages` passes assistant content through untouched |
| OpenAI | `response["choices"][0]["message"]`, `.tool_calls` | `input` comes from `json.loads(tc["function"]["arguments"] or "{}")` — the arguments arrive as a **JSON string**, not an object. Ruby added `require "json"` for exactly this |
| Gemini | `response["candidates"][0]["content"]["parts"]` | Splits `functionCall` parts from `text` parts. **Reuses the function name as the id** — Gemini assigns none |
| Ollama / OllamaCloud | `response["message"]["tool_calls"]` | Same name-as-id trick. Note the text block is added only when `message["content"]` is present **and non-empty** — Ollama returns `""` rather than omitting it |

Ruby's `response.dig("choices", 0, "message") || {}` is a safe nested lookup;
Python needs an explicit guarded walk (a small `_dig` helper, or chained
`.get(...) or {}` / index guards). Do not use bare `[...]` chains — a missing
key would raise instead of yielding the empty-response behaviour Ruby has.

OpenAI, Gemini, Ollama, and OllamaCloud also gain the **inverse**
(`assistant_message` / `assistant_parts`), used by `to_messages` when replaying
an `assistant` turn from history:

| Ruby | Python |
|---|---|
| `content.is_a?(String) ? [{"type" => "text", "text" => content}] : content` | `[{"type": "text", "text": content}] if isinstance(content, str) else content` |
| `text_blocks.map { \|b\| b["text"] }.join` | `"".join(b["text"] for b in text_blocks)` |
| `b["input"].to_json` (OpenAI only) | `json.dumps(b["input"])` — OpenAI wants arguments as a string; Ollama and Gemini pass the object through |

